title: Maintenance therapy in classical follicular lymphoma
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

[[Classical follicular lymphoma]]

* ''After R-CHOP or R-CVP:'' offer rituximab maintenance after response to prolong remission and delay subsequent treatment; an overall survival benefit has not been demonstrated. The studied schedule is 375 mg/m² IV every 8 weeks for 2 years. See [[PRIMA trial]].
* ''After bendamustine–rituximab (BR):'' maintenance can be considered, but there is no dedicated PRIMA-like comparison with observation in unselected BR responders. Indirect randomized and retrospective evidence support possible benefit; PET/MRD negativity alone is not a validated reason to omit it. See [[FOLL12 trial]], [[MAINTAIN trial (follicular lymphoma)]], and [[Hill 2019 retrospective cohort|Rituximab maintenance after BR - Hill 2019 cohort]].
* ''Obinutuzumab maintenance:'' use as part of the studied obinutuzumab induction-plus-maintenance sequence, including after obinutuzumab–bendamustine. Evidence supports the complete sequence in frontline and rituximab-refractory disease, without isolating maintenance's contribution or supporting a routine switch from rituximab solely for maintenance. See [[GALLIUM trial]] and [[GADOLIN trial]].
* ''Obinutuzumab schedule:'' 1,000 mg IV every 2 months for up to 2 years, beginning approximately 2 months after induction; stop for progression and modify for toxicity. [[Prescribing information|https://www.gene.com/download/pdf/gazyva_prescribing.pdf]]
* ''Individualize the decision:'' balance longer remission against infections, immune recovery, treatment visits, and patient preference, particularly after bendamustine. Screen for hepatitis B before anti-CD20 therapy and monitor blood counts and infections. See [[Infectious prophylaxis]].
